[{"page":1,"text":"MINUTES OF A REGULAR\n\nPLANNING COMMISSION MEETING\n\nMarch 4, 2026, at 6:00 PM\n________________\n\nATTENDANCE:\n\nCOMMISSIONERS PRESENT: David Pearce, Graden Ostler, Jordan Christensen, and\n\nMartina Huntington\n\nSTAFF PRESENT: Cache Hancey, Senior Planner; Naseem Ghandour, City Engineer and\nPublic Works Director; Anthony Fletcher, Planner; Madison Reed, Planning Technician\n\nOTHERS PRESENT: Daria Evans, Hunter Smith, Ryan Bybee, John Pettingill\n\nMotion: COMISSIONER JORDAN CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO ELECT\n\nCOMMISSIONER DAVID PEARCE AS CHAIR TEMPORE FOT THIS MEETING AT\n\n6:00PM. COMMISSIONER GRADEN OSTLER SECONDED. ALL IN FAVOR SAID YES:\n\nHUNTINGTON, PEARCE, CHRISTENSEN, AND OSTLER. THE MOTION CARRIED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\n\n1.\n\nCALL TO ORDER/INVOCATION/INSPIRATIONAL THOUGHT/PLEDGE OF\n\nALLEGIANCE\nChair Tempore David Pearce called the meeting to order. Commissioner Christensen led\nthe prayer and the pledge of allegiance.\n\n2. PRESENTATIONS/RECOGNITIONS/AWARDS/PROCLAMATIONS\n\nThere were no items submitted.\n\n3.\n\nPUBLIC COMMENTS PC\n\nResident Daria Evans welcomed the new commissioners. She asked about red curbing in\nthe Villas residence. She also asked about the impact fees for storm and land drains. She also\nasked about ADUs in a development from a business item seen tonight.\n\n4.\n\nCONSENT ITEMS\n\nPage 1 of 6; March 4, 2026, Planning Commission Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"4.1. Approval of the December 3rd, 2025 Planning Commission Meeting Minutes\nPlanning Technician Madison Reed clarified that the minutes were not from the last\nmeeting.\n\nMotion: COMMISSIONER CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO APPROVE THE\n\nDECEMBER 3RD, 2026 PLANNING COMMISSION MEETING MINUTES AT\n\n6:06PM. COMMISSIONER OSTLER SECONDED. ALL IN FAVOR VOTED YES:\n\nHUNTINGTON, PEARCE, CHRISTENSEN, AND OSTLER. THE VOTE CARRIED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\nSenior Planner Cache Hancey clarified the status of the minutes from the last meeting.\n\n5.\n\nBUSINESS ITEMS\n\n5.2.\n\n2026 Planning Commission Meeting Calendar Amendment\n\nChair Tempore Pearce noted that the suggestion was to move meetings to 7pm.\n\nDiscussion about day of the week and time ensued.\n\nSenior Planner Hancey suggested to move to 7pm and then poll to understand a\nbetter day.\n\nMotion: COMMISSIONER CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO MOVE THE\n\nMEETINGS TO 7:00PM ON THE REGULARL WEDNESDAY SCHEDULE.\n\nCOMMISSIONER HUNTINGTON SECONDED. ALL IN FAVOR VOTED YES:\n\nHUNTINGTON, PEARCE, CHRISTENSEN, AND OSTLER. THE VOTE CARRIED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\n\n5.3.\n\nPublic Hearing: The Forge Development Agreement Amendment\n\nSenior Planner Hancey suggested that this item and item 5.4. were postponed to the\nnext planning commission meeting on March 18th.\n\nCommissioner Martina Huntington asked about documents for review.\nSenior Planner Hancey noted that they will be attached in the agenda packet for that\n\nMarch 18th meeting.\n\nTechnician Reed noted that the agenda and packet are sent out the Thursday prior.\n\nMotion: COMMISSIONERR CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO MOVE ITEMS\n\n5.3. AND 5.4. TO THE MARCH 18TH PLANNING COMMISSION MEETING AT\n\n6:11PM. COMMISSIONER HUNTINGTON SECONDED. ALL IN FAVOR VOTED\n\nYES: HUNTINGTON, PEARCE, CHRISTENSEN, AND OSTLER. THE VOTE\n\nCARRIED UNANIMOUSLY.\nPage 2 of 6; March 4, 2026, Planning Commission Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"5.4. Public Hearing: Intermountain Health Development Agreement\n\nDiscussed above.\n\n5.5.\n\nPublic Hearing: Holdaway Fields Development Agreement Amendment\n\nMotion: COMMISSIONER CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO OPEN INTO A\n\nPUBLIC HEARING AT 6:11PM. COMMISSIONER HUNTINGTON SECONDED.\n\nALL IN FAVOR VOTED YES: HUNTINGTON, PEARCE, CHRISTENSEN, AND\n\nOSTLER. THE VOTE CARRIED UNANIMOUSLY.\n\nSenior Planner Hancey discussed Daria’s questions on ADUs.\nCity Engineer and Public Works Directo Naseem Ghandour noted he had answers to her\nother questions for the end of the meeting.\n\nPlanner Anthony Fletcher presented a development summary. He provided\nlocational context, site context, developer obligations, and the segments of developments.\n\nPlanner Fletcher clarified the senior housing.\n\nPlanner Fletcher discussed the requested amendment to revise the phasing plan and\npark completion timing. He noted staff discussions about impact fees. He noted the\nrecommendation to allow for 1 year for the City Council to decide on structures for the\nEast Park. Planner Fletcher presented the original phasing plan, the currently approved\nphasing plan, and the proposed phasing plan.\n\nChair Tempore Pearce clarified that there were changes on each side.\n\nPlanner Fletcher highlighted the next steps. He further discussed the impact fees\nand provided a breakdown.\n\nChair Tempore Pearce clarified that the entire structure is being changed.\n\nPlanner Fletcher continued to discuss the impact fee suggested changes.\n\nCommissioner Ostler asked about the progress on the church in this area.\n\nSenior Planner Hancey noted he will conversate off line.\n\nRyan Bybee with Cadence Homes noted that the agreement came before many staff\nwith the City. He provided history on the development and impact fees. He provided the\nprogression of agreements within the development agreement from his experience. Mr.\nBybee discussed the proposed amendments to phasing and amenities. He noted that the\ndeveloper would not to go through with the amendments if the impact fees are required to\nPage 3 of 6; March 4, 2026, Planning Commission Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"change as an additional agreement. Mr. Bybee noted that there was zero obligation on the\ndeveloper to cooperate with Heritage Park.\nChair Tempore Pearce noted that he was impressed by his presentation. He noted that\nhaving impact fees locked in that early is fairly impressive. He asked if there has been\nany compromise on the amount staff has presented.\n\nMr. Bybee noted that there has not been any open discuss on impact fees. It was\nbrought up from staff.\n\nDirector Ghandour noted he had information on water and history to share.\n\nChair Tempore Pearce asked if the developer withdrew their requested amendments.\n\nSenior Planner Hancey noted that staff did not care about phasing.\nDirector Ghandour noted his understanding and experience with the water rights and\nimpact fees. He discussed the fee schedule, water types, and park phasing.\n\nChair Tempore Pearce noted that changing in phasing has been seen many times. He\nnoted that impact fees has not been brought to this body regarding this development.\n\nResident Ms. Evans noted concerns for the TSSD fees and Orem water.\n\nDirector Ghandour noted that we have disconnected from Orem water.\n\nResident Ms. Evans noted concerns for a 300 W connection through this\ncommunity. She voiced that the City shouldn’t bare this.\n\nMotion: COMMISSIONER CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO CLOSE THE\n\nPUBLIC HEARING AT 7:02PM. COMMISSIONER OSTLER SECONDED. ALL IN\n\nFAVOR VOTED YES: HUNTINGTON, PEARCE, CHRISTENSEN, AND OSTLER.\n\nTHE VOTE CARRIED UNANIMOUSLY.\n\nPlanner Fletcher noted that the commissioner opportunity to recommend,\nrecommend with conditions, further compromise, or recommended denial. He noted that\nthe applicant may pull their application and the amendments would not be further\nconsidered.\n\nCommissioner Christensen noted comments on land use is not contributing the\n\nCity’s financial well being.\n\nMr. Bybee discussed the impact that the development brought.\n\nCommissioner Christensen discussed his point and discussed competing land uses.\n\nPage 4 of 6; March 4, 2026, Planning Commission Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"Mr. Bybee noted that if the impact fees are needed from the city, they would want\nto implement higher density through new zoning.\n\nDiscussion ensued.\n\nResident John Pettingill discussed his experience and his preference on density.\nCommissioner Christensen noted that it is in the City’s best interest to adjust the impact\nfees.\n\nMr. Bybee noted that the agreement needs to be approved from both parties. He\nfurther commented on the disagreement with the perspectives of impact fees.\n\nMr. Pettingill clarified who is responsible for what in the development.\n\nDiscussion ensued.\n\nChair Tempore Pearce noted that he supports the rephasing and feels that that\nshould be separate from the impact fees.\n\nCommissioner Christensen noted he’d make a motion to recommend that impact fees\nshould be handled separately.\n\nPlanner Fletcher clarified the recommendation.\n\nDirector Ghandour noted that Planning Commission’s recommendation is separate\nfrom staff’s recommendation.\n\nDiscussion ensued.\n\nMotion: COMMISSIONER CHRISTENSEN MOTIONED TO RECOMMEND\n\nTHE APPROVAL OF THE CHANGES FOR PHASING AND STRUCTURES WITHIN\n\nTHE PARK, BUT TO REMOVE THE IMPACT FEES TO THE CHANGES OF THE\n\nDEVELOPMENT TO CITY COUNCIL AT 7:23PM. COMMISSIONER\n\nHUNTINGTON SECONDED. ROLL CALL WENT AS FOLLOWS: OSTLER,\n\nHUNTINGTON, HUNTINGTON, AND PEARCE. THE VOTE CARRIED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\n\n6. WORK SESSION\n\nNo work items were submitted.\n\n7. STAFF AND COMMISSION REPORTS\n\nSenior Planner Hancey noted that this meeting is Technician Reed’s last Planning\n\nCommission as she will be leaving Vineyard.\n\nPage 5 of 6; March 4, 2026, Planning Commission Minutes","ocr":false},{"page":6,"text":"8.\n\nADJOURNMENT\n\nThe meeting was adjourned at 7:26pm.\n\nMINUTES CERTIFIED COMPLETE ON: March 18, 2026\n\nCERTIFIED (NOTICED) BY: /s/. Madison Reed\n\nMadison Reed, Planning Tech\n\nPage 6 of 6; March 4, 2026, Planning Commission Minutes","ocr":false}]